welcome back so we're talking about data  science and we've talked about how you  can ask questions and answer them with  data and that it's this this feedback  process so I really want now to talk  about the nature of data so the nature  of your data because this plays a big  role in what kinds of questions you can  ask and answer how you collect the data  how you store it analyze visualize the  types of models you can build with  machine learning so the nature of data  is very very critical and there's lots  of types of data out there there's a  huge variety big data sparse data and  everything in between and nowadays we  hear a lot about Big Data this is kind  of a big buzzword big data so everyone  is competing to see whose data is bigger  you know companies are buying new  equipment to measure things more  accurately so they can collect more and  more data oftentimes you know big data  has benefits so there's been a lot of  successes from Big Data so think of  Google Image Search or even Google  search in general huge success of big  data but there's a lot of times a lot of  occasions where you're collecting big  redundant data data that is not actually  adding value by just collecting more of  it and so I think it's not always  important to stay in the Big Data regime  but sometimes we want to be thinking  about what I'm gonna call smart data  this is a bio-inspired principle so I  think about you know animals a lot  insects when they're flying they're not  collecting huge data about their  environment they have very targeted  sensors they have strain sensitive  neurons on their wings a few of them  that are collecting just the right  information then they listen to these  strain sensors the smart data to inform  them about the the system they're  interacting with so sometimes big data  makes sense sometimes you want to go to  the smart data limit where you actually  learn to listen to a massive sub sample  of your data  instead of all of the data you listen to  some of it that's maximally informative  okay  so let's talk about the types of data  you have the nature of your data so  oftentimes your data you know it might  come in a big Excel spreadsheet that  happens all the time someone sends me an  Excel spreadsheet so it's not always in  a fancy database  you know you might email your colleague  and say hey I need this data to answer  this question and he emails you or she  emails you a big Excel spreadsheet okay  and in that spreadsheet there might be  numbers and letters and categories and  all kinds of messy stuff that's what you  normally find so there's numerical data  numerical data or things you know  numbers like zero point nine seven two  is an numerical data you might expect to  see they might be categorical so  categorical so for example if I have a  bunch of images of cats and dogs I might  have a label is it category A or  category B or is this part type A or  type B or type C so categorical data  comes in a few categories you know in  this case a B or C and then there's a  there's cases which are less less  clear-cut not just numbers or categories  but something that's somewhat in between  so these are called ordinal so let's say  you have a 5-star recommender system  like a rating system so I have one  through five stars it's discrete yeah  kind of like these categories but there  is an order to those five is bigger than  one so this is called ordinal and in the  real world what you're going to have our  mixed data types you're going to have  images which are millions of numerical  values millions of pixels each with a  numerical value and each image might  have a category or a few categories  there's a dog and the cats something  like that categories describing the  numerical data so that that's very  common another key distinction about the  nature of your data is whether or not  it's high dimensional or low dimensional  okay so a high dimensional data and this  is a very subjective  categorization high dimensional and low  dimensional so one person's low  dimensional might be someone else's high  dimensional but I'll give you some  extreme examples ok so high dimensional  data are things like images are  inherently there's many degrees of  freedom or dimensions used to describe a  megapixel image there's a million picked  pieces of pixel information that you use  to describe an image so if I have an  image here let's make it a cat then this  this image data is high dimensional by  nature of the fact that there's a  million pieces of information to  describe that lower dimensional I mean  to some extent for humans anything  higher than three dimensional is high  dimensional because we can see in three  dimensions but low dimensional is you  know smaller than that so maybe if I  have one measurement in time you know a  time series of one measurement or two  measurements I'll give you an example  low dimensional maybe I'm running an  experiment I'm trying to design a new  super alloy and it's super expensive it  cost me $50,000 to bake and test this  new super alloy and after this very  expensive procedure I get out four  numbers okay I get out these four  material properties that describe that  material that's kind of a low  dimensional output there's only a few  numbers that cost a lot to get and I  only get a few numbers out whereas you  know images are cheap and very high  dimensional okay so low dimensional high  dimensional that's a key a key  distinction another major distinction in  in data science in the nature of data is  whether or not your data comes with  labels so to some extent is it labeled  or not and this makes a huge difference  in terms of what machine learning  algorithms you can use to model your  data so if you have labels let's say in  this case it didn't just say it wasn't  just a cat but I knew that it came from  the category there was this label that  this is a cat and maybe someone else you  know I also have images of dogs and  someone 