k that the kind of intersection of  many or few and high and low dimensional  there's some really interesting  categories of problems  so typically neural networks for example  work really really well when you have  many examples so neural networks are  great for many examples of labeled data  not so great for a few example data okay  so that's a huge huge distinction you  know what what just general type of data  is it high D or low D does it have  labels are there many or few and then  another one I think of and this is the  last one I'm going to write down and I  think of this a lot because I work with  physical systems engineering systems  systems from science where we know  Newton's laws exist and are true and I'm  dealing with systems that are evolving  in time according to physics is your  data temporal in nature is is it kind of  temporal or does it come from physics is  there some physics or dynamics that  describe your data that's also very  important so if our my many examples  correlated in time or are they kind of  discrete examples like picture of cat  picture of dog okay is it a movie or  just a sequence of random images that's  how I think of it okay and these are  just you know some of my favorite  categorizations of the nature of data  there's a lot I'm glossing over you can  have a lot of more subtlety and mixed  types you know in the Human Genome  Project presumably they collected extra  information about that person you know  what are their diet what's their blood  pressure what's their age things like  that so you have extra information and  this all goes into practical questions  about how would you store the data  how would you collect the data how would  you what computational hardware and  architectures would you use you know  what databases would use to store the  data what machine learning algorithms  would you use to process the data can I  do it on my laptop or do I need a  supercomputer those are all very  important questions and then 